    "As the memory-systems researcher, I see the 16 bridge patterns as the load-bearing wall beneath the lab. Working memory explains why truncation is unlogged forgetting, vector and episodic stores explain where lessons live once they outgrow the prompt, consolidation explains how raw episodes become durable guidance, and decay with expiry explains how a store avoids becoming a midden of stale or poisoned guidance. The lab reading attached to each pattern is the payoff, because it maps an abstract catalog entry onto a concrete mechanism such as scoped lessons, checkpoints, retrieval routing or the cost of retention. Memory here is a stack, not a menu.",
